const faq = [
  { q: 'Where is the Corvus constellation?', a: 'Corvus lies in the southern sky, bordered by Virgo (to the north), Hydra (to the south and west), and Crater (to the west). It is best seen in spring from the Northern Hemisphere (April–May) and autumn from the Southern Hemisphere. It is a small constellation with no particularly bright stars — its four main stars (Alchiba, Gienah, Kraz, and Algorab) range from magnitude 2.6 to 4.0, forming a distinctive small quadrilateral sometimes called "the Sail."' },
  { q: 'What is the Greek myth associated with Corvus?', a: 'The most common Greek myth links Corvus to the story of Apollo and the raven. Apollo sent his raven to fetch water in a cup (the constellation Crater). The raven delayed to eat figs from a tree, then returned with a water snake (Hydra) as a false excuse. Apollo, enraged by the deception, placed the raven, the cup, and the snake in the sky — with the raven unable to drink from the cup (Crater) due to the snake (Hydra) guarding it. This explains why three adjacent constellations form a visual unit.' },
  { q: 'Is Corvus the same as the raven in other mythologies?', a: 'The constellation Corvus is an ancient Near Eastern constellation that predates Greek astronomy — Babylonian records identify a "raven" star-pattern in roughly the same part of the sky. The Greeks and Romans identified it with their own raven myths (Apollo\'s raven). The constellation\'s identification as "the Crow" or "the Raven" (both translate as corvus/korax in Latin/Greek) predates the specific Apollo myth that became attached to it.' },
