    <h1>Corvus: The Raven Constellation and the Apollo Myth</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Corvus (Latin: crow or raven) is one of the 48 constellations listed by Ptolemy in the second century CE and remains one of the 88 modern constellations. It represents Apollo's raven — the bird blamed for bringing bad news about the nymph Coronis and punished by being placed in the sky where it can never drink from the adjacent cup (constellation Crater), perpetually thwarted by the water-snake (Hydra).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Stars of Corvus</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Corvus is a small constellation of four main stars forming a compact quadrilateral, sometimes compared to a sail or a small sailing boat. The stars and their names:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Gamma Corvi (Gienah)</strong> — the brightest star in Corvus at magnitude 2.6. The name comes from Arabic <em>janāḥ al-ghurāb</em>, "the wing of the raven."</li>
      <li><strong>Beta Corvi (Kraz)</strong> — magnitude 2.6; the second brightest. Name of uncertain origin.</li>
      <li><strong>Delta Corvi (Algorab)</strong> — magnitude 3.0. Name from Arabic <em>al-ghurāb</em>, "the raven."</li>
      <li><strong>Alpha Corvi (Alchiba)</strong> — magnitude 4.0; the designated alpha star despite not being the brightest. Name from Arabic <em>al-khiba'</em>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Several Arabic star names in Corvus confirm the raven/crow identification predates Greek astronomy — the Arabic names derive from the pre-Islamic and early Islamic astronomical tradition that preserved and transmitted earlier Near Eastern star lore.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Three-Constellation Myth</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Corvus belongs to a visually connected trio of constellations — Corvus (the Raven), Crater (the Cup), and Hydra (the Water Snake) — all lying along or near the ecliptic in the southern sky. The standard myth as recorded by several ancient sources (including Ovid's <em>Fasti</em> and the mythographic compilation attributed to Eratosthenes called the <em>Catasterismi</em>) links all three:<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <p>Apollo sent his raven to fetch water from a spring in a cup (Crater). The raven, spotting a fig tree, waited for the figs to ripen and then ate his fill. Returning late, he carried a water-snake (Hydra) in his talons and blamed the snake for blocking the spring. Apollo, who could see the deception, placed all three — raven, cup, and snake — in the sky, condemning the raven to thirst forever because Hydra always blocks his access to Crater.</p>
    <p>This myth is an aetiology (origin story) for the visual grouping of three real star-patterns. See also the fuller account of the Apollo–raven myth at <a href="/lore/world/apollo-and-the-raven/">Apollo and the Raven</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Babylonian Origins</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The identification of this area of the sky as "the raven" predates Greek astronomy. Babylonian astronomical texts from the second millennium BCE identify a star-pattern in this region as <em>UGA</em> (the raven), and the star now known as Gienah was associated with this bird figure in pre-Greek astronomy. The Greek constellation Corvus is therefore an adaptation of an older Near Eastern tradition, not a Greek invention.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Bird Behind the Myth</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Latin <em>corvus</em> and Greek <em>korax</em> refer primarily to the Common Raven (<em>Corvus corax</em>), though both words can also be used for large crows. The raven was familiar across the ancient Mediterranean world — a conspicuous, clever bird that would have been a natural choice for a mythological character associated with Apollo's intelligence and the consequences of deception. See <a href="/species/common-raven/">Common Raven</a> for the bird itself.</p>
  </section>

